iverdance git@github-quiverdance:Sys-KU/TieredServe.git || git -C /root/projects/tiered-serve remote add quiverdance git@github-quiverdance:Sys-KU/TieredServe.git; }\\\" C-m\\ntmux send-keys -t \\\"$SESSION:0.0\\\" \\\"rm -f /tmp/tiered-serve-bootstrap.ok && /opt/tiered-serve-startup-patches/bootstrap_latest.sh && touch /tmp/tiered-serve-bootstrap.ok && source /root/projects/tiered-serve/.venv/bin/activate && cd /root/projects/tiered-serve\\\" C-m\\n\\n# Pane 1: 오른쪽 위\\ntmux split-window -h -t \\\"$SESSION:0.0\\\"\\n\\n# Pane 2: 오른쪽 아래\\ntmux split-window -v -t \\\"$SESSION:0.1\\\"\\n\\n# Pane 1: ssh + tiered-serve\\necho \\\"[+] Pane 1: will exec into container after $SLEEP_TIME seconds...\\\"\\ntmux send-keys -t \\\"$SESSION:0.1\\\" \\\"sleep $SLEEP_TIME && sudo docker exec -it $CONTAINER_NAME /bin/bash\\\" C-m\\ntmux send-keys -t \\\"$SESSION:0.1\\\" \\\"while pgrep -f '[b]ootstrap_latest.sh' >/dev/null; do sleep 1; done; test -f /tmp/tiered-serve-bootstrap.ok && /usr/sbin/sshd && source /root/projects/tiered-serve/.venv/bin/activate && cd /root/projects/tiered-serve\\\" C-m\\n\\n# Pane 2: parent \\necho \\\"[+] Pane 2: will exec into container after $SLEEP_TIME seconds...\\\"\\ntmux send-keys -t \\\"$SESSION:0.2\\\" \\\"sudo sh -c 'echo 3 > /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches'\\\" C-m\\n# tmux send-keys  \\\"$SESSION:0.2\\\" \\\"cat whchoi/c\\\" C-m\\n# tmux send-keys -t \\\"$SESSION:0.2\\\" \\\"sleep $SLEEP_TIME && sudo docker exec -it $CONTAINER_NAME /bin/bash\\\" C-m\\n# tmux send-keys -t \\\"$SESSION:0.2\\\" \\\"source /root/projects/tiered-serve/.venv/bin/activate && cd /root/projects\\\" C-m\\n\\necho \\\"[+] Attaching to tmux session...\\\"\\ntmux attach -t \\\"$SESSION\\\"\\n# CSR garbage policies\\n\\nThese experiments use the original CD3 CSR storage and decoder. Dedicated KV\\ncapacity, GPU staging capacity, and attention kernels are unchanged. The\\nstarting point is remote `origin/main` at\\n`19a15c5d66d14481acb7e00c5e6f371d8ffb0964`.\\n\\nBoth performance suites accept `--cd3-kv-csr-policy append|compact|reuse`.\\nThe default is `append`; the other policies require `--cd3-kv-storage csr`.\\nStandalone callers set `TIERED_SERVE_KV_CD3_CSR_POLICY`.\\n\\nThe subsequent [CPU optimization](cd3-cpu-path.md) is selectable with\\n`--cd3-kv-cpu-path legacy|optimized` (default: `optimized`). The original\\npolicy-only measurements in [the earlier report](cd3-csr-results.md) used\\nthe legacy CPU path; [the CPU report](cd3-cpu-results.md) separates stages.\\n\\n| Policy | Retirement | New encoded entries |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| `append` | Mark old words as garbage | Append; retain existing capacity-pressure rebuilds |\\n| `compact` | Repack the remaining live entries after each physical retirement batch | Repack if overwriting records creates garbage; otherwise append |\\n| `reuse` | Coalesce freed ranges and trim a free tail | Best-fit hole first, then append; rebuild on capacity/fragmentation failure |\\n\\nThe `compact` policy publishes new pinned entries and offsets. Older plans keep\\ntheir original tensors until H2D completes. It preserves allocated capacity\\nbut reduces `used_words`, which controls contiguous entries transfers. Returning\\nmemory to the OS is not required to remove garbage from H2D.\\n\\nThe `reuse` policy uses indices ordered by address and by `(size, address)`.\\nAdjacent free ranges merge. A remainder smaller than eight 64-bit words stays\\nwith its allocation, allowing at most 56 bytes of internal slack per such\\nallocation. Logical entry counts remain exact. The free tail is removed from\\n`used_words` immediately; interior holes and internal slack remain part of a\\ncontiguous entries transfer until they are reused or a rebuild occurs.\\n\\nRetirement only changes allocation metadata in `reuse` mode. The existing\\nencoder waits for the layer's preceding H2D before overwriting any body or\\nentries bytes. If an encoder is pending, physical retirement remains deferred\\nuntil that writer stops. This applies to both new policies.\\n\\n## Measurement\\n\\n`cd3_csr_garbage_bytes` counts dead entries and internal slack actually included\\nin CD3 H2D submissions. It is a subset of `cd3_gap_bytes`: the latter also\\nincludes entries of live but inactive blocks. If the existing capacity fallback\\npacks selected entries, its transferred garbage is zero. A policy does not\\nclaim to remove live-but-inactive data.\\n\\nThe serving suite's `--kv-byte-metrics` records per-forward byte counters\\nwithout enabling GPU timing. All compared policies must use the same option.\\nByte totals cover all server forwards, whereas the client's request trimming\\nonly affects client latency statistics.\\n\\nAt worker shutdown, `CD3 CSR totals` logs compaction count, bytes repacked,\\nbytes placed in holes, bytes appended, and bytes retired. Its\\n`sum_peak_used_bytes` is the sum of individual layer/side high-water marks,\\nnot a simultaneously measured process-memory peak.\\n\\nThe bounded comparison uses real requests from the existing Azure trace:\\n\\n```bash\\nfor policy in append compact reuse; do\\n  bash scripts/serving/run_performance_suite.sh \\\\\\n    --codecs cd3 --modes kvcomp \\\\\\n    --cd3-kv-storage csr --cd3-kv-csr-policy \\\"$policy\\\" \\\\\\n    --cd3-kv-execution serial \\\\\\n    --chunks 8192 --request-rates 4 --max-num-seqs 64 \\\\\\n    --dedicated-kv-blocks 2432 \\\\\\n    --num-prompts 192 --skip-first-n-requests 16 \\\\\\n    --skip-last-n-requests 16 --repeats 1 --kv-byte-metrics \\\\\\n    --output-root \\\"benchmark_results/cd3_csr/$policy\\\"\\ndone\\n```\\n\\nCompaction removes all dead entries but copies the live entries repeatedly.\\nReuse avoids most movement but can retain scattered holes. A possible follow-up\\nis reuse with occasional compaction when measured wasted transfers justify the\\ncopy cost. A fixed threshold is a heuristic, not an optimality guarantee;\\nthe two policies above isolate the tradeoff before adding that combination.\\